# Testing Rules — Project Equinox (AI TDD)

## TDD is Strictly Enforced
1. Write the test file FIRST
2. Run the test — it must FAIL (red)
3. Write the implementation to make it pass (green)
4. Refactor if needed
5. Never write implementation before tests exist

## Test File Structure
Every test file uses table-driven tests. This is the Go standard.

```go
func TestHeuristicMatchesSimilarTitles(t *testing.T) {
    tests := []struct {
        name       string
        marketA    models.Market
        marketB    models.Market
        wantMatch  bool
        wantMinConf float64
    }{
        {
            name: "identical titles match",
            marketA: models.Market{Title: "will democrats control the house 2026"},
            marketB: models.Market{Title: "will democrats control the house 2026"},
            wantMatch:   true,
            wantMinConf: 0.95,
        },
        {
            name: "similar wording same event matches",
            marketA: models.Market{Title: "democrats win house majority 2026"},
            marketB: models.Market{Title: "will democrats control house after midterms 2026"},
            wantMatch:   true,
            wantMinConf: 0.75,
        },
        {
            name: "unrelated events do not match",
            marketA: models.Market{Title: "will bitcoin exceed 100k in 2026"},
            marketB: models.Market{Title: "will democrats control house 2026"},
            wantMatch:   false,
            wantMinConf: 0.0,
        },
    }

    for _, tt := range tests {
        t.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {
            result := heuristic.Match(tt.marketA, tt.marketB)
            if result.IsMatch != tt.wantMatch {
                t.Errorf("Match() = %v, want %v", result.IsMatch, tt.wantMatch)
            }
            if tt.wantMatch && result.Confidence < tt.wantMinConf {
                t.Errorf("Confidence = %v, want >= %v", result.Confidence, tt.wantMinConf)
            }
        })
    }
}
```

